    <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="what-title">
      <div class="wrap split split--top">
        <div>
          <p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Overview</p>
          <h2 class="title" id="what-title" data-fx="wipe">What is <span class="grad-text">anemia?</span></h2>
          <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">Your red blood cells carry oxygen from your lungs to every part of your body. They use a protein called hemoglobin to do this. When hemoglobin drops below the healthy level, your organs get less oxygen. This is anemia.</p>
          <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">Anemia is very common in our state. About 48 out of every 100 women and two out of three young children in Karnataka have anemia (NFHS-5). Many people live with it for years, thinking tiredness is “normal”. It is not, and with timely anemia treatment in Bangalore, you don’t have to put up with it.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box" data-fx="fade">
          <h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-flask"/></svg>When is it anemia?</h3>
          <p class="box__sub">According to the World Health Organization, you have anemia if your hemoglobin is below:</p>
          <table class="htable"><thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>Hemoglobin below</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adult men</td><td><b>13 g/dL</b></td></tr><tr><td>Adult women (not pregnant)</td><td><b>12 g/dL</b></td></tr><tr><td>Pregnant women</td><td><b>11 g/dL</b></td></tr><tr><td>Children (6 months to 5 years)</td><td><b>11 g/dL</b></td></tr></tbody></table>
          <p class="box__sub">Anemia can be mild, moderate or severe. Even mild anemia is worth checking, because it is often an early sign of another problem.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="sec sec--tint" aria-labelledby="sym-title">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="head">
          <div><p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Signs &amp; symptoms</p><h2 class="title" id="sym-title" data-fx="wipe">Common signs of <span class="grad-text">anemia</span></h2></div>
          <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">Anemia often develops slowly, so many people don’t notice it at first. Watch for:</p>
        </div>
        <ul class="sym sym--3"><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-activity"/></svg><b>Constant tiredness</b><span>Feeling tired or weak all the time</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-drop"/></svg><b>Paleness</b><span>Pale skin, lips or inner eyelids</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-lungs"/></svg><b>Breathlessness</b><span>On stairs or while walking</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-heart"/></svg><b>Fast heartbeat</b><span>A fast or uneven heartbeat</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-cells"/></svg><b>Dizziness</b><span>Headaches or feeling light-headed</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-thermo"/></svg><b>Cold hands &amp; feet</b><span>Even in normal weather</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-scale"/></svg><b>Brittle nails, hair fall</b><span>Weak nails and thinning hair</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-doc"/></svg><b>Poor focus</b><span>Trouble concentrating at work or school</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-flask"/></svg><b>Unusual cravings</b><span>Ice, mud or chalk, a sign of low iron</span></li></ul>
        <div class="cols2 mt">
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade"><h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-users"/></svg>Anemia symptoms in women</h3><p class="box__sub">Women are more likely to have anemia because of monthly periods, pregnancy and breastfeeding. Heavy periods, constant tiredness and hair fall are common clues.</p></div>
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:.08s"><h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-child"/></svg>Anemia symptoms in children</h3><p class="box__sub">Children may look pale, eat poorly, get irritable, fall sick often or grow more slowly. Dr. Neema Bhat is a US board-certified pediatrician and pediatric hematologist, so children with anemia get specialised care.</p></div>
        </div>
        <p class="alert" data-fx="fade"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-heart"/></svg><span><b>See a doctor urgently</b> if you have chest pain, fainting, severe breathlessness, or black or bloody stools.</span></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="cause-title">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="head">
          <div><p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Causes</p><h2 class="title" id="cause-title" data-fx="wipe">What causes <span class="grad-text">anemia?</span></h2></div>
          <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">Anemia is not one disease. It is a sign that something is wrong. The common causes are:</p>
        </div>
        <ul class="conds conds--4"><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><b><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-flask"/></svg>Low iron, vitamin B12 or folate</b><p>From diet, poor absorption, or vegetarian diets low in B12.</p></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><b><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-drop"/></svg>Blood loss</b><p>Heavy periods, piles, stomach ulcers, or slow bleeding in the gut.</p></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><b><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-heart"/></svg>Pregnancy</b><p>The growing baby needs extra iron.</p></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><b><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-shield"/></svg>Long-term illnesses</b><p>Kidney disease, thyroid problems, diabetes, arthritis or infections.</p></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><b><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-users"/></svg>Inherited blood conditions</b><p>Thalassemia and sickle cell disease.</p></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><b><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-cells"/></svg>Red cells breaking down early</b><p>Called hemolytic anemia.</p></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><b><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-marrow"/></svg>Bone marrow problems</b><p>Aplastic anemia, or blood cancers like leukemia and myeloma.</p></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><b><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-pill"/></svg>Certain medicines</b><p>Some long-term medicines or chemotherapy.</p></li></ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="sec sec--tint" aria-labelledby="risk-title">
      <div class="wrap cols2">
        <div class="box" data-fx="fade">
          <h3 id="risk-title"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-users"/></svg>Who is at risk of anemia?</h3>
          <p class="box__sub">Anyone can get anemia, but the risk is higher for:</p>
          <ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Women with heavy periods, and pregnant or breastfeeding women</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Babies, young children and teenagers, who grow fast and need more iron</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Vegetarians and vegans, who may get too little vitamin B12</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Adults over 65</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>People with kidney disease, diabetes, thyroid problems, IBD or celiac disease</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>People who have had stomach or weight-loss surgery</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>People who take painkillers (NSAIDs), acid-reducing tablets or metformin for a long time</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>People with worm infections, which can cause slow blood loss</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Anyone with a family history of thalassemia or sickle cell disease</span></li></ul>
        </div>
        <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:.08s">
          <h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-shield"/></svg>What happens if anemia is left untreated?</h3>
          <p class="box__sub">Mild anemia may feel like simple tiredness, but over time it can cause serious problems:</p>
          <ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Heart strain:</b> the heart pumps harder to deliver oxygen, which can lead to an enlarged heart or heart failure.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Pregnancy problems:</b> higher risk of preterm birth, low birth weight and heavy bleeding during delivery.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Slower growth in children:</b> delayed growth, poor school performance and frequent infections.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Weak immunity and low energy:</b> that affect work and daily life.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>A missed diagnosis:</b> anemia can be the first sign of hidden bleeding, kidney disease or a blood cancer.</span></li></ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="types-title">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="head head--center">
          <div><p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Types</p><h2 class="title" id="types-title" data-fx="wipe">Types of anemia <span class="grad-text">we treat</span></h2></div>
          
        </div>
        <div class="cards3"><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-drop"/></svg><h3>Iron deficiency anemia</h3><p>The most common type. The body lacks iron to make hemoglobin.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-drop"/></svg><h3>Vitamin B12 &amp; folate deficiency</h3><p>Megaloblastic and pernicious anemia. Low B12 or folate makes red cells large and weak. Common in vegetarians.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-drop"/></svg><h3>Anemia of chronic disease</h3><p>Caused by long-term kidney, liver or inflammatory illness.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-drop"/></svg><h3>Hemolytic anemia</h3><p>Including autoimmune. Red cells are destroyed faster than the body can replace them.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-drop"/></svg><h3>Thalassemia</h3><p>An inherited condition where the body makes faulty hemoglobin.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-drop"/></svg><h3>Sickle cell anemia</h3><p>Inherited; red cells become sickle-shaped and block blood flow.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-drop"/></svg><h3>Aplastic anemia</h3><p>The bone marrow stops making enough blood cells.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-drop"/></svg><h3>Sideroblastic anemia</h3><p>The body cannot use iron properly to make hemoglobin.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-drop"/></svg><h3>Anemia in pregnancy &amp; children</h3><p>Special care for mothers-to-be, babies and teenagers.</p></div></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="sec sec--tint" aria-labelledby="dx-title">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="head">
          <div><p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Diagnosis</p><h2 class="title" id="dx-title" data-fx="wipe">How is anemia <span class="grad-text">diagnosed?</span></h2></div>
          <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">Finding the right cause is the most important step. Dr. Neema Bhat may advise some of these anemia blood tests:</p>
        </div>
        <ol class="olist"><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><span class="olist__n">01</span><div><b>Complete Blood Count (CBC)</b><p>Checks hemoglobin and the size and number of red cells.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><span class="olist__n">02</span><div><b>Peripheral blood smear</b><p>Looks at your blood cells under a microscope.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><span class="olist__n">03</span><div><b>Iron studies</b><p>Serum iron, ferritin and TIBC to measure iron stores.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><span class="olist__n">04</span><div><b>Vitamin B12 and folate levels</b><p>To find vitamin deficiencies.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><span class="olist__n">05</span><div><b>Reticulocyte count</b><p>Shows whether your bone marrow is making new cells.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><span class="olist__n">06</span><div><b>Hemoglobin electrophoresis / HPLC</b><p>To check for thalassemia or sickle cell.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><span class="olist__n">07</span><div><b>Kidney, liver and thyroid tests</b><p>To look for long-term illnesses behind the anemia.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><span class="olist__n">08</span><div><b>Stool test or endoscopy</b><p>If hidden bleeding is suspected.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><span class="olist__n">09</span><div><b>Bone marrow test</b><p>Only when needed, for aplastic anemia or blood cancers.</p></div></li></ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="tx-title">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="head">
          <div><p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Treatment</p><h2 class="title" id="tx-title" data-fx="wipe">Anemia treatment options <span class="grad-text">in Bangalore</span></h2></div>
          <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">Your anemia treatment in Bangalore depends on the type and cause of your condition. Dr. Neema Bhat creates a plan for you that may include:</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cards3 cards3--5"><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-pill"/></svg><h3>Iron tablets or syrup</h3><p>The usual iron deficiency anemia treatment for mild to moderate cases.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-syringe"/></svg><h3>IV iron infusion</h3><p>A faster option when tablets cause stomach upset, don’t work, or iron is needed quickly, for example in pregnancy or before surgery.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><h3>Vitamin B12 &amp; folic acid</h3><p>B12 injections or tablets and folic acid for B12 and folate deficiency.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-users"/></svg><h3>Treating the root cause</h3><p>Such as heavy periods, gut bleeding, thyroid or kidney problems, with the right specialist.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-pill"/></svg><h3>Immune-calming medicines</h3><p>For autoimmune hemolytic anemia.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-syringe"/></svg><h3>Blood transfusion</h3><p>Only for severe anemia or emergencies.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-syringe"/></svg><h3>Transfusion &amp; iron chelation</h3><p>Regular transfusions with iron chelation for thalassemia.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-award"/></svg><h3>Immunosuppressive therapy</h3><p>For aplastic anemia.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-marrow"/></svg><h3>Bone marrow (stem cell) transplant</h3><p>A possible cure for thalassemia, sickle cell disease and severe aplastic anemia. <a href="/bone-marrow-transplant">Learn more</a></p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-apple"/></svg><h3>Diet &amp; lifestyle advice</h3><p>Simple Indian food choices to keep your hemoglobin healthy.</p></div></div>
        <p class="alert alert--warn" data-fx="fade"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-shield"/></svg><span><b>Do not start iron tablets on your own for a long time.</b> Too much iron can harm the body, and the tablets may hide a more serious cause.</span></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="sec sec--tint" aria-labelledby="food-title">
      <div class="wrap cols2">
        <div class="box" data-fx="fade">
          <h3 id="food-title"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-sun"/></svg>Foods that help treat and prevent anemia</h3>
          <p class="box__sub">Food supports treatment but rarely fixes anemia alone. Include:</p>
          <ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Iron-rich foods:</b> ragi, green leafy vegetables (palak, methi, drumstick leaves), dals, rajma, chana, jaggery, dates, eggs, chicken, fish and meat.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Vitamin C with meals:</b> lemon, amla, orange or guava help your body absorb iron.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Vitamin B12 sources:</b> milk, curd, paneer, eggs and fish.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Avoid tea or coffee within one hour of meals:</b> it reduces iron absorption.</span></li></ul>
        </div>
        <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:.08s">
          <h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-users"/></svg>Prevention &amp; carrier testing</h3>
          <p class="box__sub">India’s national Anemia Mukt Bharat programme gives free iron and folic acid tablets to children, teenagers, and pregnant and breastfeeding women through government health centres.</p>
          <p class="box__sub">Inherited anemias such as thalassemia cannot be prevented with food. A simple thalassemia carrier test (HPLC) before marriage or pregnancy shows whether a couple could pass on the condition. If both partners are carriers, each child has a 1 in 4 chance of thalassemia major, so early counselling helps families plan.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>


