        <div class="faq2__list"><details class="qa" open><summary><span class="qa__n">01</span><span class="qa__q">Which doctor should I see for anemia in Bangalore?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">A hematologist (blood specialist) is the right doctor for anemia that is moderate, severe, keeps coming back, or does not improve with iron tablets. Dr. Neema Bhat is a US-trained hematologist in Bangalore who treats all types of anemia in adults and children.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">02</span><span class="qa__q">What hemoglobin level is considered anemia?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">The WHO defines anemia as hemoglobin below 13 g/dL in men, below 12 g/dL in non-pregnant women, and below 11 g/dL in pregnant women and young children.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">03</span><span class="qa__q">Can anemia be cured permanently?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">Yes, most anemias caused by low iron, B12 or folate can be cured once the cause is corrected. Inherited types like thalassemia need long-term care, and some can be cured with a bone marrow transplant.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">04</span><span class="qa__q">How long does anemia treatment take?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">Most people with iron deficiency feel better in 2 to 3 weeks, and hemoglobin returns to normal in about 2 months. Treatment usually continues for a few more months to rebuild iron stores.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">05</span><span class="qa__q">What is the fastest way to treat anemia?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">The fastest safe treatment depends on the cause. For iron deficiency, an IV iron infusion can raise iron levels much faster than tablets. Blood transfusion is used only for severe anemia or emergencies.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">06</span><span class="qa__q">Can anemia be treated without a blood transfusion?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">Yes. Most patients are treated with iron tablets, IV iron, vitamin B12 or folic acid, and by fixing the root cause. Transfusions are needed only in severe or special cases.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">07</span><span class="qa__q">Does seeing a hemato-oncologist for anemia mean I have cancer?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">No. A hemato-oncologist treats all blood problems: common ones like anemia as well as blood cancers. Most anemia is caused by low iron or vitamins. Seeing a blood specialist simply means the cause is found faster and treated correctly.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">08</span><span class="qa__q">Can anemia come back after treatment?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">It can, if the underlying cause, such as heavy periods, a poor diet or slow bleeding in the gut, is not fixed. Regular follow-up blood tests help prevent this.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">09</span><span class="qa__q">How much does anemia treatment in Bangalore cost?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">Most anemia treatment, such as blood tests and iron or vitamin supplements, is affordable. Costs rise only for IV infusions, transfusions or specialised treatments. The clinic can share an estimate after your first consultation.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">10</span><span class="qa__q">Can children and pregnant women get anemia treatment?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">Yes. Dr. Neema Bhat is a US board-certified pediatrician and hematologist, and she treats anemia in babies, children, teenagers and pregnant women with safe, age-appropriate care.</p></details></div>
